# 22.13 Design Decisions

## Why Separate Preferences from Profiles?

Authentication data changes infrequently, whereas application settings evolve regularly.

Separating these concerns keeps the authentication model clean while allowing rapid expansion of configurable features.

---

## Why Create Default Preferences Automatically?

Automatically generating a default preference record ensures that every authenticated user has a complete configuration immediately after account creation.

This eliminates null checks throughout the application and simplifies backend logic.

---

## Why Store AI Preferences Globally?

Most users expect consistent AI behavior across conversations.

Global preferences provide sensible defaults while still allowing conversation-specific overrides when needed.

---

## Why Store UI Preferences in the Database?

Persisting preferences in PostgreSQL allows users to experience a consistent interface across multiple devices and browser sessions.

---

# 22.14 Business Rules

- Every authenticated user has exactly one preferences record.
- Preference records cannot exist without a corresponding user profile.
- Preferences are automatically created during account registration.
- Users may modify their own preferences at any time.
- Preference changes never affect existing conversations unless explicitly synchronized.
- AI preference values are applied only to newly created chat sessions.
- Preference updates are immediately reflected in the user interface.
- Users cannot access or modify another user's preferences.
- Preference records are permanently removed when the associated profile is deleted.

---

# 22.15 Future Expansion

The current schema is designed to support future personalization features without structural redesign.

Potential enhancements include:

- Multiple AI provider selection
- Preferred embedding model
- Default retrieval depth
- Default response style
- Citation format selection
- Language preferences
- Accessibility settings
- Keyboard shortcut customization
- Custom prompt templates
- Workspace personalization
- Plugin preferences
- Voice AI configuration

These features can be integrated by extending the existing preference model.

---

# 22.16 Summary

The `user_preferences` table centralizes all configurable user settings within KnowledgeOS.

By separating personalization data from authentication and application logic, the system provides a scalable and maintainable approach to managing AI behavior, interface customization, notification preferences, and privacy controls.

This design ensures a consistent user experience across devices while remaining flexible enough to accommodate future personalization capabilities without impacting the core database architecture.

