# 24. Database Summary

---

# 24.1 Complete Database Architecture

KnowledgeOS follows a hybrid storage architecture that combines a relational database for transactional consistency with a vector database for semantic retrieval.

```text
                    ┌──────────────────────────────┐
                    │         Frontend             │
                    │         (React)              │
                    └──────────────┬───────────────┘
                                   │
                                   ▼
                    ┌──────────────────────────────┐
                    │        FastAPI Backend        │
                    └──────────────┬───────────────┘
                                   │
                 ┌─────────────────┴──────────────────┐
                 │                                    │
                 ▼                                    ▼
      ┌──────────────────────┐            ┌─────────────────────┐
      │     PostgreSQL       │            │      ChromaDB       │
      │                      │            │                     │
      │ Profiles             │            │ Embeddings          │
      │ Folders              │            │ Chunk Text          │
      │ Documents            │            │ Vector Metadata     │
      │ Document Chunks      │            │                     │
      │ Chat Sessions        │            └─────────────────────┘
      │ Chat Messages        │
      │ User Preferences     │
      │ Insights             │
      └──────────────────────┘
```

The relational database stores structured application data, while ChromaDB stores semantic vector representations used by the Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipeline.

---

# 24.2 Database Responsibilities

## PostgreSQL

PostgreSQL serves as the **source of truth** for all application data.

It manages:

- User authentication extensions
- Folder organization
- Document metadata
- Chunk metadata
- Chat history
- User preferences
- AI-generated insights
- Audit timestamps
- Relationships
- Constraints
- Indexes

The database guarantees transactional consistency and strong referential integrity.

---

## ChromaDB

ChromaDB stores semantic representations of document chunks.

Each vector record contains:

- Embedding vector
- Chunk text
- Chunk UUID
- Document UUID
- User UUID
- Version
- Retrieval metadata

This enables fast semantic similarity search without repeatedly querying PostgreSQL.

---

